  "goodToKnow": "Der Diktator is a dictator; die Diktatur is a dictatorship. Germany experienced two: the Nazi dictatorship (1933–1945) and the SED regime in the GDR, often called a Diktatur too. Note the stress shift in the plural: dik-TAA-tor, but dik-ta-TOH-ren. Das Diktat is a dictation, a classic German school exercise for spelling. From Latin dictator, an emergency magistrate in ancient Rome. Plural: die Diktatoren."
